<td id="S4.T3.2.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">0.3</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.1.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.4.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">0.304</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.1.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.4.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">0.304</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.1.4.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.4.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">0.315</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.2.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">G-EVAL-MASE</span></th>
<td id="S4.T3.2.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">0.857</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.1.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.5.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">0.92</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.1.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.5.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">0.857</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.1.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.5.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">0.865</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.1.5.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.5.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">0.913</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Model Interpretability.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One of Super-Linear’s key strengths lies in its interpretability through expert selection. Unlike deep MoE models such as Time-MoE, where multi-layered complexity hinders transparent analysis, Super-Linear employs a single expert layer, enabling direct examination of the softmax outputs. In Fig. <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4.3 Expert Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Super-Linear: A Lightweight Pretrained Mixture of Linear Experts for Time Series Forecasting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, we present the learned expert distributions for ETT, Weather, and Electricity. For example, the Electricity dataset, which is sampled hourly, predominantly activates the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/24</annotation></semantics></math> expert, corresponding to hour/day periodicity. The Weather dataset tends to favor the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/144" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>144</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/144</annotation></semantics></math> expert (10 min/day), and also often selects the naïve expert, likely indicating the presence of noise or low-frequency dynamics. These distributions expose clear links between sampling rates, seasonal patterns, and expert activation, offering valuable insights into Super-Linear’s decision-making behavior.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15105v1/top_k_experts_selection.svg" id="S4.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/217;" width="476" height="217"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Top-k expert selection comparison. Performance across varying top-<math id="S4.F8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> expert settings. Blue and red vertical lines indicate the <math id="S4.F8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> values used during pretraining and inference, respectively.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Top-k Experts Ablation.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Another insightful analysis involves evaluating performance as a function of the top<span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span><math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> experts hyperparameter for the sparse MoE. We conduct experiments with varying <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in{3,6,9,12,15}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in{3,6,9,12,15}</annotation></semantics></math>, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Model Interpretability. ‣ 4.3 Expert Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Super-Linear: A Lightweight Pretrained Mixture of Linear Experts for Time Series Forecasting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. A key feature of Super-Linear is its ability to adapt the expert selection at inference time by recalibrating the expert distribution for any chosen <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq N</annotation></semantics></math>. Namely, we can pretrain on one <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and evaluate on another <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. In this experiment, we assess the performance of a Super-Linear model pretrained with <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=12</annotation></semantics></math>, comparing both inference-time adjustments (red curve) and retraining with different <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> values (blue curve). Results show dataset-dependent behavior, with a preference for the retrained setup for ETTh1, ETTm2, and Weather; however, adjusting the top<span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span><math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> at inference can be advantageous in some inference-time cases—for example, on ETTh1 and ETTm1, selecting <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3</annotation></semantics></math> outperforms the original <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=12</annotation></semantics></math> configuration, despite the model being trained with <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=12</annotation></semantics></math>. This highlights the practicality and adaptability of Super-Linear for zero-shot forecasting tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Theoretical Analysis</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide a theoretical analysis of the gating mechanism through the lens of the <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">bias-complexity tradeoff</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shalev-Shwartz &amp; Ben-David, 2014</a>)</cite>, yielding bounds on the frequency approximation error. This framework helps assess the strengths and limitations of our frequency-specialized approach. Specifically, it decomposes the error of the ERM algorithm into a frequency-focused approximation error <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\text{app}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mtext>app</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\text{app}}</annotation></semantics></math>, representing bias from limited model capacity, and estimation error <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\text{est}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mtext>est</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\text{est}}</annotation></semantics></math>, arising from overfitting due to model complexity. By analyzing this tradeoff, we obtain theoretical insights into the robustness of the model and the gating mechanism. Assuming <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y(X)</annotation></semantics></math> share the same periodicity, formally: <math id="S5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{X}=I^{Y}=I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mi>X</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{X}=I^{Y}=I</annotation></semantics></math>, and for some <math id="S5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> we then get,</p>
<table id="S5.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|Y(X)-\hat{Y}(X)\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|Y(X)-\hat{Y}(X)\|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{E}_{\text{app}}+\mathcal{E}_{\text{est}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mtext>app</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mtext>est</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{E}_{\text{app}}+\mathcal{E}_{\text{est}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\sqrt{E_{\perp}}+\gamma\lVert X\rVert_{2}\|\beta-G\|_{1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>E</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mi>X</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\sqrt{E_{\perp}}+\gamma\lVert X\rVert_{2}\|\beta-G\|_{1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math> bounds the <em id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(frequency)<span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>approximation error</em>: the signal
energy outside the span of the <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> expert frequencies.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The second term, which bounds the <span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">estimation error</span>, penalises how far the learned gating
<math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is from the optimal <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>.
If training makes <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\beta-G\|_{1}\!\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\beta-G\|_{1}\!\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>,
the total error collapses to the intrinsic
residual <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{E_{\perp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><msub><mi>E</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{E_{\perp}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To ease notation, <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math> represent <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(X)</annotation></semantics></math>,and <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\perp}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\perp}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
Adding more frequency and complementary experts reduces approximation error but risks overfitting, while too few experts lead to underfitting, reflecting the bias–complexity trade-off <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shalev-Shwartz &amp; Ben-David, 2014</a>)</cite>. Incorporating natural frequencies <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Dataset Training Details ‣ Appendix C Datasets ‣ Super-Linear: A Lightweight Pretrained Mixture of Linear Experts for Time Series Forecasting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a> introduces a meaningful inductive bias, ensuring sufficient representative experts to cover diverse datasets while controlling complexity to prevent overfitting. In <a href="#A1.SS7.SSS0.Px2" title="𝐋_𝐢𝐧𝐩𝐮𝐭&gt;𝟓𝟏𝟐. ‣ A.7 Varying Input Lookbacks ‣ Appendix A Supplementary Experiments and Results ‣ Super-Linear: A Lightweight Pretrained Mixture of Linear Experts for Time Series Forecasting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.7</span></a>, we provide the proof and provide an analysis with a stricter assumption on the shared periodicity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Super-Linear is a sparse mixture-of-experts framework for zero-shot and full-shot time series forecasting that combines frequency-specialized linear experts with a spectral routing function to achieve efficient, accurate, and interpretable generalization across diverse benchmarks. While effective and scalable, its reliance on linear experts limits performance on highly nonlinear or chaotic series, suggesting future extensions with nonlinear or hybrid experts and more advanced routing strategies to enhance adaptability in complex settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
